@page "/frame"

<CatHead Title="Shell" Tier="@CatHead.Tier1">
    The page shells: the full three-part layout, the two reduced ones, and the rhythm inside them.
    Each part stands on its own, so an app combines the ones it needs; these are combinations to start from.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-information-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">The frame is markup, not components</span>
        There is no
        <code>&lt;AppShell&gt;</code> and there will not be one — copy the markup below, and report a
        frame problem against the library rather than overriding it locally.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Frame.FullShell" Title="Full shell" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        <code>.layout</code> is the flex row that fills the viewport — shown here at a fixed
        height — <code>.content</code> is the column beside the sidebar, and <code>.page</code> is
        the only scroll container. Width is two knobs on <code>.sidebar</code>,
        <code>--sidebar-width</code> (220px) and <code>--sidebar-width-collapsed</code> (56px);
        nothing else about the frame changes with them.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Frame.BareShell" Title="Bare shell" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        <code>.bare-layout</code> — topbar and body, no sidebar, for the page that comes before
        there is a workspace to navigate: choosing one, accepting an invitation, a first-run step.
        Where there is nothing to offer at all use <code>.auth-layout</code> below;
        <a href="/common-pages">Common pages</a> has the finished pages.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Frame.AuthLayout" Title="Auth layout" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        Sign-in, sign-out, &ldquo;check your e-mail&rdquo;, an invitation — one centred card and no
        navigation. <code>.auth-card</code> is capped rather than stretched.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Frame/FullBleedLayout.html" Title="Full-bleed layout">
    <p>
        For content that owns the whole area below the header — a map, a board, a diagram editor.
        Use it <em>instead of</em> <code>.page</code> and never nested inside it, and put
        <code>.full-layout-fill</code> on the child that takes the height that is left.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Frame.PageRhythm" Title="The rhythm between page sections" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        Every direct child of <code>.page</code> after the first gets <code>--page-gap</code> above
        it, so a card after a card needs no class and no <code>style="margin-top"</code>. Set
        <code>--page-gap: 0</code> on <code>.page</code> to manage the rhythm yourself, or
        <code>.sedna-mt-0</code> on the one child that should sit flush; inside
        <code>.card-body</code> there is no equivalent — reach for
        <a href="/utility-layout"><code>.sedna-col</code></a> with a gap step.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Frame.PageFoot" Title="A footer at the bottom of a short page" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        <code>.page--fill</code> makes the page a flex column, so a <code>.page-foot</code> &mdash;
        a footer, a build line &mdash; sits at the bottom of a page that is shorter than the
        viewport rather than under its last paragraph, and a lone
        <a href="/empty-state"><code>.empty-state--page</code></a> centres itself between the top
        and the foot. Opt in per page: a flex column stops sibling margins collapsing, which the
        block page relies on. This site's own pages use it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Frame/NarrowScreens.html" Title="Narrow screens">
    <p>
        Add <code>.layout--responsive</code> to <code>.layout</code> and the sidebar becomes the
        rail below 900px, where it is forced — so hide a collapse toggle at that width or accept
        the no-op. <code>.layout--compact-at-sm</code> moves that width to 560px and
        <code>.layout--compact-at-lg</code> to 1200px. A topbar outside a <code>.layout</code> opts in with
        <code>.topbar--responsive</code> on the <code>.topbar</code> instead. Put
        <code>.sidebar-wide</code> on your own content in the sidebar that has no place in the
        rail — it goes whenever the labels do, toggled or forced — and <code>.topbar-wide</code> on
        your own header content that can go below 560px.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Frame/PhoneFlow.html" Title="On a phone, the document scrolls">
    <p>
        <code>.page</code> is the frame's only scroll container, and that is the one thing a
        phone's browser cannot see: Safari collapses its toolbar only while the <em>document</em>
        scrolls, so a page scrolling inside a box keeps the full toolbar on screen for ever.
        <code>.layout--flow</code> hands the scrolling to the document below 560px &mdash; the
        shell grows with its content, the topbar sticks under the notch, a sidebar sticks to the
        top of its column and a bottom bar to the bottom of the viewport &mdash; and changes
        nothing above it. This site uses it. Keep the box for a page that must never move under
        its header, a full-bleed map or an editor. Safari on iOS 26 can leave its viewport offset
        after the page has scrolled to its end or the keyboard has closed, which draws the bottom
        bar and the header in the middle of the page; <code>Sedna.UI.js</code> notices and nudges
        it back, once, and only then.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Frame/Kiosk.html" Title="A wall screen">
    <p>
        <code>.kiosk</code> is a screen nobody operates: the whole viewport, never scrolled, no
        cursor, and the same canvas about 1280px wide scaled up to every screen — 1.5 at 1080p, 2 at
        1440p, 3 at 4K — so a page designed once reads the same on each; <code>--kiosk-zoom</code>
        sets a factor of your own. No scrollbar is drawn inside it, because what moves is moved by
        the app. <code>.kiosk-strip</code>, <code>.kiosk-stage</code> and <code>.kiosk-side</code>
        keep their areas when one is empty; <code>--kiosk-side</code> is the side column's width.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Frame/SkipLink.html" Title="Skip link">
    <p>
        The first focusable thing on the page, jumping past the navigation to the content —
        off-screen rather than hidden, so it stays focusable. <code>tabindex="-1"</code> on
        <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> is not optional: without it the jump only scrolls and focus stays
        back in the navigation. Neither is <code>Sedna.UI.js</code>: under the host page's
        <code>&lt;base href="/"&gt;</code> a bare <code>#main</code> means the start page, and the
        script is what keeps the jump here. This site has one &mdash; press Tab on any page.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Frame/Regions.html" Title="Moving between regions">
    <p>
        <kbd>F6</kbd> moves focus to the next region of the frame and <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F6</kbd> to the
        previous, wrapping at both ends: every landmark &mdash; <code>header</code>, <code>nav</code>,
        <code>main</code>, <code>aside</code>, <code>footer</code> or a landmark role &mdash; every
        <code>.topbar</code>, <code>.sidebar</code> and <code>.bottombar</code>, and an open
        <code>data-modeless</code> drawer or sheet, outermost only and in document order. A hidden region, an
        inert one and an off-canvas drawer are skipped. Focus lands on the region's first control, or on the
        region itself when it has none. While a modal dialog is open the key is left to the browser. Nothing
        to write: <code>Sedna.UI.js</code> does it for the markup above, and
        <code>sednaUi.nav.moveRegion(1)</code> does it from code. Press <kbd>F6</kbd> on this page.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
